# 2 The Basilisk language {#ch:language}

Basilisk C is C99 with a few additions. This chapter lists them, with the
rules that are easy to get wrong. Everything here was checked against the
source tree or by compiling a test program.

## Compiling with qcc

~~~bash
export BASILISK=$HOME/basilisk/src
export PATH=$PATH:$BASILISK
qcc -O2 -Wall case.c -o case -lm              # serial
qcc -O2 -Wall -fopenmp case.c -o case -lm     # OpenMP (OMP_NUM_THREADS=n ./case)
~~~

`qcc` translates the file to C99, runs a static check of physical
dimensions, and passes every option it does not know to the C compiler. Its
own options include:

| Option | Effect |
|:---|:---|
| `-grid=octree` | use this grid instead of the one included in the file |
| `-catch` | stop with a message on floating-point errors (NaN, division by zero) |
| `-events` | print a trace of the events as they run |
| `-source` | write the generated C file (`_case.c`) and stop |
| `-disable-dimensions` | skip the dimension check |
| `-Wdimensions` | report dimension errors as warnings only |

For MPI, generate the C source and compile it with the MPI compiler:

~~~bash
qcc -source -D_MPI=1 case.c
mpicc -O2 -std=c99 -D_XOPEN_SOURCE=700 -D_GNU_SOURCE=1 _case.c -o case -lm
mpirun -np 8 ./case
~~~

> **The dimension checker**
>
> `qcc` tries to infer the physical dimension of every constant and
> reports combinations that do not agree. It sometimes reports errors for
> correct code, typically in expressions that mix a dimensional function call
> and a bare number such as `cond ? u(x) : 0.`. If the code is right,
> compile with `-disable-dimensions` instead of changing the physics to
> please the checker. It also catches real mistakes. While preparing this
> guide, `exp(-100.*(sq(x - 0.5) + sq(y - 0.5)))` was rejected because
> the exponent has units of length squared; writing
> `exp(-(sq(x - 0.5) + sq(y - 0.5))/sq(0.1 [1]))` states that the width
> 0.1 is a length and passes. The bracket annotations, `0.1 [1]`,
> `L0 = 8. [1]` or `DT = 0.1 [0,1]`, declare dimensions (here
> length, and time) for the checker and have no other effect. `qcc`
> reports a dimension error but still builds the program.

## The structure of a program

The order of the `#include` lines matters, because each file can
depend on the ones before it:

1.  the grid (`grid/multigrid.h`, `grid/octree.h`, ...). If no
    grid is included, `qcc` uses `grid/quadtree.h`;

2.  `embed.h` if there is a solid body (it must come before the
    solver);

3.  the solver (`navier-stokes/centered.h`, `saint-venant.h`,
    ...);

4.  modules that add to the solver (`two-phase.h`,
    `navier-stokes/conserving.h`, `tension.h`, `reduced.h`,
    `tracer.h`, `diffusion.h`, ...);

5.  your own headers.

Then come global parameters and boundary conditions, `main()`, and the
events. `main()` sets the domain and parameters and calls
`run()`, which runs the time loop until no event is left to run.

## Fields

Fields are declared with an empty bracket and are allocated on the whole
grid:

~~~literatec
scalar s[];          // one value per cell
vector u[];          // u.x, u.y (and u.z in 3D), cell-centred
face vector uf[];    // uf.x on x faces, uf.y on y faces, ...
tensor T[];          // T.x.x, T.x.y, ...
~~~

A field declared at file scope exists for the whole run; a field declared
inside a function or event is allocated there and freed at the closing
brace. Fields can also be created and freed by hand:

~~~literatec
scalar w = new scalar;
...
delete ({w});
~~~

A *constant* field takes no memory and has the same value everywhere:

~~~literatec
const face vector muc[] = {1e-3, 1e-3};   // a constant face field
mu = muc;
mu[] = {1e-3, 1e-3};                      // the same, in one line
~~~

Fields carry *attributes*, for example `f.sigma` (surface tension of
an interface), `s.gradient` (slope limiter, such as
`minmod2`), `s.nodump` (leave out of dump files), or
`s.refine` and `s.prolongation` (interpolation on the tree).

## Loops over the grid

All computation on fields happens inside loops that visit the grid:

| Loop | Visits |
|:---|:---|
| `foreach()` | every leaf cell |
| `foreach_face()` | every face, in each direction |
| `foreach_face(x)` | only the faces normal to $x$ |
| `foreach_vertex()` | every cell vertex |
| `foreach_level(l)` | every cell at level `l` |
| `foreach_dimension()` | repeats a block for $x$, $y$, $z$ with indices rotated |

Inside a loop, `x`, `y`, `z` are the coordinates of the cell
centre (or of the face centre in `foreach_face()`), `Delta` is the
cell size, `level` the tree level and `dv()` the cell volume
(including the solid fraction, if any). Neighbours are reached by relative
index: `s[1]`, `s[-1]`, `s[0,1]`, `s[1,1]`, and so on, up
to the number of ghost layers (one by default, two with `embed.h`).

`foreach_dimension()` writes the $x$ version of a block and gets the
others free:

~~~literatec
foreach()
  foreach_dimension()
    g.x[] = (s[1] - s[-1])/(2.*Delta);   // also g.y[] = (s[0,1] - s[0,-1])/(2.*Delta), ...
~~~

**Reductions.** A loop that accumulates into a variable must
declare it, so that the result is correct with OpenMP and MPI:

~~~literatec
double vol = 0., umax = 0.;
foreach (reduction(+:vol) reduction(max:umax)) {
  vol += f[]*dv();
  if (fabs(u.x[]) > umax) umax = fabs(u.x[]);
}
~~~

Without the reduction clause, the result is wrong with OpenMP or MPI.

## Events {#sec:events}

An event is a function with a schedule:

~~~literatec
event name (schedule) { ... }
~~~

| Schedule | Runs |
|:---|:---|
| `i = 0`, `t = 0` | once, at the first step |
| `i++` | every step |
| `i += 10` | every 10 steps |
| `t += 0.1` | every 0.1 time units (the time step is shortened to land on these times) |
| `t = 0; t <= 5; t += 0.1` | from 0 to 5 every 0.1 |
| `i += 10; i <= 1000` | every 10 steps up to step 1000 |
| `t = {0, 1, 2.5}` | at the listed times |
| `t = end` | once, after the last step |

Three rules decide how a run behaves. All three were confirmed with small
test programs for this guide.

> **When does a run stop?**
>
> `run()` continues while at least one event still has a scheduled time or
> iteration *with an upper limit*. Events with no limit (`i++`,
> `t += 0.1`) do not keep the run alive. A program whose only events are
> unlimited stops after the first step. Give at least one event an end, for
> example `event end (t = 10)` or `event logfile (t += 0.1; t <= 10)`.
> An event that returns a non-zero value stops the run immediately; this is
> the way to stop on a condition (convergence, a blow-up).

> **Event names: overloading**
>
> Several events with the same name all run, at the slot of the first
> definition, in *reverse* order of definition: the one defined last runs
> first. Solvers define named slots (`init`, `properties`,
> `acceleration`, `projection`, `adapt`, ...) and modules add to
> them this way. Events with new names run after the solver's events, in the
> order they appear in the file.

> **Floating-point event times**
>
> `t += 0.1; t <= 0.3` does *not* run at $t = 0.3$: the third time is
> $0.1 + 0.1 + 0.1 = 0.30000000000000004 > 0.3$. Use a limit slightly above
> the last wanted time (`t <= 0.3 + 1e-6`) or a list
> (`t = {0, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3}`).

## Boundary conditions

Conditions are assignments at file scope (or in `main()` before
`run()`):

~~~literatec
u.n[left]  = dirichlet (1.);     // normal velocity on the left side
u.t[top]   = dirichlet (0.);     // tangential velocity (u.r: second tangential, 3D)
p[right]   = dirichlet (0.);
pf[right]  = dirichlet (0.);     // the half-step pressure needs the same condition
f[left]    = neumann (0.);
u.n[embed] = dirichlet (0.);     // on an embedded solid
periodic (right);                // left and right sides are periodic
~~~

The sides are `left`, `right`, `bottom`, `top`,
`back`, `front` and `embed`. Inside the expression, `x`,
`y`, `z` and `t` can be used, and `s[]` is the interior
cell next to the boundary. [Chapter 18](18_boundary) explains how each condition becomes
a ghost-cell value.

## Global variables you will use

| Name | Meaning |
|:---|:---|
| `t`, `i`, `dt` | current time, iteration number, time step |
| `L0`, `X0`, `Y0`, `Z0` | domain size and lower-left corner (`size()`, `origin()`) |
| `N` | number of cells per side for `init_grid()` / `run()` |
| `DT` | maximum time step |
| `CFL` | Courant number (0.8; 0.5 with VOF) |
| `TOLERANCE`, `NITERMAX` | multigrid tolerance ($10^{-3}$) and maximum cycles (100) |
| `mgp`, `mgu` | statistics of the last pressure and viscous solves |
| `grid->tn` | total number of leaf cells |
| `pid()`, `npe()` | MPI rank and number of processes |

